# The parens' grammar — implemented: whole-content pointer/URL stays the plain hyperlink,
# anything else parses as the field kseq.
Link targets
description: the parens hold the fragment's fields - a keyless yamlover pointer or URL is the link
- >
  The parenthesized part of a [fragment token](*::documents:marklower:grammar) holds the
  fragment's **fields** - and its most common field is the **link**: a keyless **yamlover
  expression**. Canonically that is a *pointer* - the same `*`-sigiled grammar as
  [pointers](*::language:pointers) everywhere else, addressing the app's **instance space** (the
  space the tree, the breadcrumbs, and the URL navigate):
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: table>
  header:
    - Target
    - 'Anchored at'
      width: 2
  - ['`*:: a: b`', 'the **project root** (the directory yamlover was served)']
  - ['`*: a: b`, `*: 2`', 'the **document** the link appears in (the nearest yamlover entity)']
  - ['`*..: sib`', 'the **parent** of the mapping the prose belongs to']
  - ['`*name`', 'the mapping the prose belongs to (**current** scope)']
  - ['`scheme://...`, `mailto:...`', 'an ordinary **external** link, opened in a new tab']
- >
  Inside prose the pointer is written **compact** (`*::a:b`) - a `: `-spaced spelling inside a
  bare scalar line would re-split the line as a key and value, and compact is valid in every
  context. A position is the **bare integer** segment (`*:2`, `*::a:0`); the retired bracket
  index reads forever as an alias, written never.
- >
  **Classification precedence.** When the WHOLE parens content classifies as a single pointer or
  URL under the rules above, it IS the link - today's plain hyperlink, unchanged. Otherwise the
  content parses as the field kseq (`key: value` pairs, comma-separated, a keyless pointer/URL
  among them being the link). The collision case is a head that looks like both - `(mailto: ...)`
  the URL versus `(description: ...)` the field - and the pointer/URL reading wins wherever it
  parses; a head that classifies neither way is a doctor-visible suspect, never silent prose.
- >
  **Presentational containers are transparent frames.** A `bullets`/`numbered` item and a table
  cell exist to render, not to scope: `*name` and `*..: x` there mean exactly what they mean in
  a plain `- >` block - the wrapper hands its own frame through. A *structural* cell (an
  untagged container cell folds as a chapter) is a real frame and resets normally. A link that
  still resolves to nothing is a **doctor warning** (`link/dead-target`), logged at startup and
  on every reconcile, and renders visibly broken - never as silent plain text.
- >
  The **bare colon form** - `(::a:b)`, `(:a:b)` - is the same pointer without its sigil, and
  reads **forever** as an alias; the sigiled form is what the engine emits when a
  [move](*::server) rewrites a link. The legacy slash spellings (`/a/b`
  document-relative, `//a/b` project-rooted) still navigate but are frozen - written never.
  Anything else does not resolve, and the value renders as plain text.
  `examples/69-marklower-links.yo` is a tour of the flavours.
- >
  A `&...` spelling belongs to the token's **`[]` part**, not the parens: it is the fragment's
  own [bookmark](*::language:pointers:bookmarks) - an alias, or an
  [onto](*::annotations) membership (`[&:: yamlover: ontos: colors: yellow: -: text]()`). The
  old "reserved `&` target" TODO is spent on exactly this.
- >
  One seam decides what the parens mean - `parseLinkTarget` and the token scanner beside it,
  shared verbatim by the client's `resolveLink` (navigation) and the engine's move planner
  (rewriting) - so a spelling that navigates is exactly a spelling a move keeps alive.
- >
  **This law is marklower's** (and format-less prose's). A `.md` or `.adoc` file links in ITS
  OWN format - relative file paths, made navigable GitHub-style by the markup renderers - and
  the yamlover engine neither scans, rewrites, nor doctor-checks them: a move may break such a
  link, and the author fixes it. *Engine-managed md/adoc links are a far-future feature.*
